On Thursday, February 8, 2018 5:13:24 PM CET Vuille, Martin (Martin) wrote:
> I would like to collect call-graphs on my ARM target and examine them on my
> x86_64 host. I have no debug info on the ARM target; it is available on the
> x86_64 host.
> 
> On the ARM, I am passing the '--call-graph dwarf' option to 'perf record'.
> As I understand it, this collects user stacks for each event and the
> unwinding is done by 'perf report' using DWARF debug info.
> 
> When I look at the data with 'perf report', most of the call stacks contain
> "bogus" addresses that are in data (e.g., stack) or unmapped areas of the
> process address space. (This is pure C/C++ code, there's no JITing that I
> know of.)
> 
> What I am unclear about is whether DWARF is DWARF or whether there are "ARM
> DWARF" and "x86_64 DWARF". And if the latter is true, what do I need to do
> to interpret "ARM DWARF" on my x86_64 host?

Afaik this is not yet implemented in perf report when you use libunwind. When 
you use elfutils instead of unwinding, it could work if I'm not mistaken.

Independently from that, hotspot [1] should support the above usecase just 
fine.

[1]: https://github.com/KDAB/hotspot

